From Uncertainty to Innovation: My Journey into Computer Engineering

Searching for Direction in Pre-Medical Studies

My academic journey began with F.Sc Pre-Medical, a decision shaped more by expectation than by personal understanding. Like many students in Pakistan, I selected this field without fully exploring my interests or long-term goals. At the time, it seemed like a safe and socially respected academic path. Medicine was something families encouraged, teachers pointed toward, and society rewarded with approval. So I followed that current without questioning it too deeply. But as my studies progressed through those two years, I realized something was genuinely missing. Although I worked hard and remained dedicated to my coursework, I struggled to feel connected to the subjects. Biology, Chemistry, and the memorization-heavy curriculum felt like obligations rather than explorations. I was going through the motions but not growing in the way I wanted.

The learning process in Pre-Medical felt repetitive and heavily focused on memorization rather than creativity or logical exploration. I often found myself questioning whether I truly belonged in this field. While my classmates appeared confident about becoming doctors or pursuing medical careers, I sat with a quiet uncertainty about my own future. This was not laziness — I was attending classes, completing assignments, and preparing for exams. But none of it sparked genuine curiosity. I would sit in a lecture and feel like an observer rather than a participant. That feeling is difficult to explain to someone who has never experienced it, but it is deeply uncomfortable, especially when you are investing years of your life into a path that does not feel like yours.

Those two years became a period of slow but important self-reflection. I began asking harder questions — not just what I was studying, but why, and whether this was truly where I was meant to go. I started recognizing the difference between effort and alignment. You can be hardworking in a field that is not right for you, and you will still feel unfulfilled. That realization planted the first seeds of change, even though I did not yet know where my path would lead or what it would cost me to find out.

The Turning Point: Challenges, Risks, and Persistence

A major turning point came when a friend encouraged me to attempt the ECAT entry test. At first, I treated it as an experiment rather than a serious career decision. I had no formal preparation in engineering subjects, and appearing for the exam felt more like curiosity than commitment. My performance was not strong, but the experience introduced me to something I had not felt in a long time — genuine intellectual interest. The analytical thinking and problem-solving involved in the exam captured my attention in a way that biology textbooks never had. I left that exam hall not discouraged by my result but intrigued by the process.

Curious about this new direction, I began studying more seriously for a second attempt at ECAT. I spent time going over mathematics, physics, and logical reasoning concepts that I had largely avoided during my Pre-Medical years. Despite the improved effort, my score remained lower than I had hoped, and that result hit my confidence hard. I began to doubt whether I was truly capable of making this transition. For a period, I convinced myself that my chances were too slim to pursue and decided not to apply for admission. The gap between where I was and where I needed to be felt impossible to close.

But life has a way of offering second chances to those who have not entirely given up. Admissions reopened unexpectedly, giving students another opportunity to apply. Choosing to try again was one of the most difficult decisions I have ever made. The process was filled with obstacles — slow websites, technical issues, failed form submissions, and the ticking pressure of approaching deadlines. At the same time, emotional stress at home due to my exam results made the situation even heavier. There were moments when it felt easier to simply accept the path I was already on. But something kept pushing me forward. Late nights, repeated attempts, and a refusal to let frustration become surrender — that phase taught me the most important lesson of my early academic life. Resilience is not built in comfortable moments. It is built precisely when quitting feels easier than continuing.

Discovering Purpose Through Computer Engineering

My persistence was finally rewarded when I secured admission to UET Lahore, Faisalabad Campus in Computer Engineering. The moment I received confirmation, something shifted inside me that I had not felt before — a genuine sense of excitement about what was ahead. This was not just an academic achievement. It was the end of a long period of uncertainty and the beginning of something that felt right. For the first time, the path I was on felt like my own.

As I began my studies, I encountered the faculty and courses that would shape my thinking in fundamental ways. One of the most influential people I met during this journey was Dr. Bilal Ahmad, a professor at UET Lahore's Faisalabad Campus. Dr. Ahmad is not simply a lecturer who delivers course material and moves on. He is a researcher and expert in artificial intelligence, machine learning, and deep learning — someone who actively trains ML models on real-world problems and brings that experience into the classroom. What struck me about his approach was his emphasis on purpose. He often spoke about choosing problems that actually matter, and his preference for medical datasets in his research reflects that value — medical data is collected with high precision and accuracy, and the insights drawn from it have real consequences for real people.

Being introduced to that kind of thinking early in my degree changed how I approached learning. Computer Engineering, I was discovering, was not just about writing code or understanding systems. It was about developing the capacity to think clearly, solve problems creatively, and build things that serve a purpose beyond the academic exercise. Every subject began to feel connected to something larger, and that connection is what had been missing during my Pre-Medical years.

Looking back now, I understand that my journey was never defined by perfect decisions. It was defined by the willingness to question, to risk, and to keep moving forward even when the outcome was uncertain. Moving from pre-medical confusion to engineering clarity taught me that failure and uncertainty are not obstacles standing between you and your destination — they are part of the road itself. Today, I carry forward the lessons of perseverance, self-belief, and the courage to take risks. The path I never planned turned out to be exactly the one I was meant to walk. You can learn more about Dr. Bilal Ahmad and his academic work on his Google Scholar profile.

